I have created the following plan after thorough exploration and analysis of the codebase. Follow the below plan verbatim. Trust the files and references. Do not re-verify what's written in the plan. Explore only when absolutely necessary. First implement all the proposed file changes and then I'll review all the changes together at the end.

## Observations

The X3-lang compiler pipeline is structurally sound: `x3-lang/crates/x3-lexer` → `x3-lang/crates/x3-ast` → `crates/x3-parser` → `crates/x3-hir` → `crates/x3-mir` → `x3-lang/compiler` (lowering + emitter) → `x3-lang/vm` (executor + verifier). The AST already has `BridgeDecl`, `AtomicSwapDecl`, `CrossChainStrategy`, and `ProposalDecl`. The VM has `ATOMIC_BEGIN/END/ROLLBACK`, `EVM_CALL`, `SVM_CALL`, and `SIMD_ADD_VV` opcodes. The `BridgeAdapter` trait exists but only has `DryRunBridge` (formerly `MockBridge`). The `FEATURE_REGISTRY.toml` tracks readiness scores (most at 0). The 50 capabilities spec exists in `x3-lang/spec/x3-unique-capabilities.md` as documentation only — none are wired end-to-end.

## Approach

Implement in six sequential phases that mirror the spec's own integration path. Each phase builds on the previous: first harden the compiler pipeline so all 50 capabilities have a real parse/lower/emit path, then wire the VM execution layer, then connect the runtime pallets, then implement the profitable contract primitives on top. This avoids building application-layer contracts on an incomplete language foundation.

